.TH SCENE 1
.SH NAME
scene, scenedemo \- show a 2-D scene in a window; drive a scene with a demo
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wm/scene
[
.I dir
]
.PP
.B scenedemo
[
.B -n
.I ticks
] [
.B -d
.I ms
] [
.I dir
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Scene
shows the scene in
.I dir
(default
.BR /mnt/scene )
in a window: entities, features and layers in a geo or xy frame, with
trails, headings, stale dimming and labels that do not overlap, drawn
by
.IR scene (2).
The directory may be a
.IR scenefs (4)
mount, when the view is live and shared: the window follows the
server's camera and writes its own pan, zoom and selection back to its
.BR ctl ,
so every viewer, and any agent, sees the same picture.
A plain directory of stanza files works too, with a local camera.
.PP
It is an ordinary window: it runs under
.IR wm (1),
in a Lucifer activity, where the activity's agent can picture it with
its
.B window
tool, or in a Matrix
.B app
region:
.IP
.EX
top app /dis/wm/scene.dis /mnt/scene
.EE
.PP
Drag pans, the wheel zooms about the pointer, a click selects;
.BR + ,
.B -
zoom,
.BR h ,
.BR j ,
.BR k ,
.B l
pan,
.B f
fits; button 3 gives a menu.
.PP
.I Scenedemo
writes a synthetic field survey \(em rovers mowing lanes, a relay, a
base and its radio range \(em as records to
.IB dir /log
(default
.BR /mnt/scene ),
one tick per second of scene time, every
.I ms
milliseconds (default 200; 0 as fast as possible), for
.I ticks
ticks (default: forever).
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
mkdir -p /mnt/scene
mount -c {scenefs} /mnt/scene
scenedemo &
wm/scene
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/wm/scene.b
.br
.B /appl/cmd/scenedemo.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR scenefs (4),
.IR scene (2),
.IR wmsrv (2)
